#This is the common and recommended configuration. Your archived logs are generated #into the FRA (Location A), but you have RMAN configured to back up to a different #device, such as: 
#A separate non-FRA disk mount point (Location B).
#A tape library via an SBT (System Backup to Tape) channel.
#In this case, the command works exactly as intended:
#RMAN reads the archive logs from the Source (Location A/FRA).
#It writes the backup pieces to the Destination (Location B/Tape).
#Upon successful completion of each backup piece, the original source file is deleted #from Location A/FRA.



#Space Management: The primary purpose is to free up disk space in the archive log #destination areas immediately after the logs are safely backed up to another location #(often tape or a different disk system).
#Single Copy Deletion: If you have multiple copies of the same archived log sequence #number (due to archiving to multiple destinations), DELETE INPUT only removes the #specific physical file that was used as the source for the backup. The other copies in #different destinations remain untouched.
#Contrast with DELETE ALL INPUT: If you had specified DELETE ALL INPUT, RMAN would #delete every copy of that log sequence number across all archiving destinations after #backing up just one copy of the log. 
#In short, this command is a standard housekeeping task to move archived logs from the #online disk storage to a backup medium and reclaim disk space.
#This is the common and recommended configuration. Your archived logs are generated #into the FRA (Location A), but you have RMAN configured to back up to a different #device, such as: 
#A separate non-FRA disk mount point (Location B).
#A tape library via an SBT (System Backup to Tape) channel.
#In this case, the command works exactly as intended:
#RMAN reads the archive logs from the Source (Location A/FRA).
#It writes the backup pieces to the Destination (Location B/Tape).
#Upon successful completion of each backup piece, the original source file is deleted #from Location A/FRA.



#Space Management: The primary purpose is to free up disk space in the archive log #destination areas immediately after the logs are safely backed up to another location #(often tape or a different disk system).
#Single Copy Deletion: If you have multiple copies of the same archived log sequence #number (due to archiving to multiple destinations), DELETE INPUT only removes the #specific physical file that was used as the source for the backup. The other copies in #different destinations remain untouched.
#Contrast with DELETE ALL INPUT: If you had specified DELETE ALL INPUT, RMAN would #delete every copy of that log sequence number across all archiving destinations after #backing up just one copy of the log. 
#In short, this command is a standard housekeeping task to move archived logs from the #online disk storage to a backup medium and reclaim disk space.


RUN {
    BACKUP ARCHIVELOG ALL DELETE INPUT;
}
